The output code is really nasty, making it hard to order the output
and unclear exactly what's going on sometimes.  And like the grid-bug
stuff, there are a lot of scattered checks for very special-case
behavior that would probably be more cleanly handled with callbacks on
the monster/object in question. (even worse, there are a few flags
which are abused to mean multiple things depending on the object type
rather than having separate flags)

IMO, explicitly setting things like the slimed state and then the
"need to redraw bottom line" flag all over the place would be better
handled with a set_slimed macro or something to avoid code duplication
(and distraction to the reader).

Probably about half the gotos used should be expunged as they don't
help make the code clearer--for instance, I'd prefer setting the
attack type explicitly before switching on it (setting AT_CLAW to
AT_WEAP beforehand and so forth if a weapon is wielded) rather than
the backwards gotos in uhitm.c; that'd keep the AT_CLAW case clearer
(it's just stuff that happens for claw attacks) and it'd make it
clearer up top which attack types are overriden by weapons. It'd just
keep the attack-type specific stuff together under the case statements
and the general "what kind of attack is used" stuff together up top.

Note that I'm not rabidly anti-goto; the no_unwear/smell gotos in
zap.c, for instance, seem fine (though the makecorpse gotos in zap.c
is a bit much).

There are a few places where a bit more explicit naming could make
things clearer without requiring hugely verbose names (e.g. rnl has
been the source of much confusion in posts on rgrn; making it rnluck
or something similar could have saved much of that).

Basically, there are just a lot of little details that could make the
code easier on people who are unfamiliar with it.

But like I said, it's not the worst code I've seen, by far.

